\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why these objectives are not interchangeable}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{post-training}

\p{A demonstration states which response to imitate. A comparison states
which of two responses a judge selected. A reward model predicts a scalar
encoding of comparisons. Process supervision locates judgments inside a
response. Each signal excludes information available to another.}

\p{Their losses also induce different policy movements. Supervised loss
raises likelihood on recorded targets. A preference loss depends on paired
alternatives and its comparison model. KL regularization limits movement
relative to a named reference. None of these facts alone determines whether
the resulting policy improves under an independent evaluator.}

\p{The first theorem questions should therefore be conditional. Which
feedback channels identify a useful ordering? When does a scalar reward
represent that ordering? Which regularizer preserves useful support? These
questions draw on \citef{christiano2017deep}, \citef{ouyang2022training},
\citef{rafailov2023direct}, and \citef{lightman2023verify}.}
